  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 100 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nSynoptic Evolution: \n\nModels are in good agreement that a shortwave will lift across\nthe Northern Plains Wednesday into Thursday, followed by another\nstronger trough late in the week. This morning's model runs \nfollow the previous forecast cycle's trend towards keeping the\nWednesday/Thursday shortwave north of our area, with the\nattendant surface cold front not reaching this far south and\neast. The stronger late week trough that follows does seem to\npush far enough south to impact our area, with indications of a\ncold frontal passage next weekend bringing the end of our \nabove-normal temperatures.\n\n\nTemperatures:\n\nConfidence remains high that a warming trend will emerge through\nthe work week, with temperatures jumping from upper 50s to low\n60s on Tuesday to upper 60s and low 70s by Thursday and Friday.\nAs a trough aloft pushes across the western US tonight into\nTuesday, the ridging in the low-levels gets displaced eastward,\nincreasing the southerly flow bringing warm air northward \nacross the Southern Plains. With this amplified WAA beginning on\nTuesday, Wednesday will begin the noticeable warm-up, with the\nwarmest day of the week appearing to be Friday at this point in\ntime. Overnight lows will also increase, with the cooler terrain\nof the eastern Ozarks seeing lows in the 40s during the latter\nhalf of the week, while warmer areas to the west see overnight\nlows in the upper 40s to near 50 degrees Thursday and Friday\nnights.\n\n\nMoisture/Rain Chances:\n\nMoisture will see a widespread increase by Thursday, with dew \npoints increasing into the 40s and even low 50s within the\nsouth-southwesterly flow / warm air advection regime. \n\nThe late week trough currently brings 15-45% rain chances \nthrough on Saturday, which remains steady with previous forecast\ncycles. Uncertainty in the amplitude and track of the second \ntrough will drive future changes to rain chances, but with a \nmoist air mass in place for a couple days prior to the wave \nmoving across the central US, we will be more primed for some \nrainfall compared to other shortwaves that have come through \nrecently. However, if the trough ends up coming through \nweaker/with less southward extent than currently forecasted, \nrain chances could trend lower with future forecast cycles. \n\nQPF amounts still appear overall low, with deterministic NBM\nguidance providing a swath of around 0.05\" across the CWA from\nsouthwest to northeast. LREF 90th percentile values are\nspatially consistent with this \"swath\" and show reasonable\nhigh-end rainfall scenarios as around 0.4\", while 25th\npercentile QPF amounts drop to 0. This would indicate that the\nmost likely scenario is very light rainfall amounts, with the \n\"high end\" accumulations remaining below a half inch, while \nmany models continue to show most areas remaining dry.",
